Lifecycle-aware borrowed texture binding caching
================================================

Source proposal: [Rendering.BorrowedTextureCaching.Proposal.md](Rendering.BorrowedTextureCaching.Proposal.md)
Status: approved design; implementation has not started. All tasks below remain open.
Parent task: docs/PBR.BaselineShading.todo / Preserve texture binding caching for
lifecycle-aware borrowed rendering inputs.

Goal and governing contracts
  Preserve known texture-unit bindings for eligible borrowed allocations and avoid repeated metadata
  acquisition. Use existing GpuTexture inputs, resource guards, cloud publication certificates and
  StateCache. Allocation continuity and current binding state are separate requirements for skipping
  a bind; frame/content validity remains an independent prerequisite for drawing.
  The external producer retains native storage ownership. Retiring a borrowed reference must never
  delete, enqueue deletion of, or transfer the native texture. Raw integer sampler inputs remain
  conservative wherever reliable lifecycle coverage is unavailable.
  Scope covers native cloud-map inputs, primary/visibility and liquid depth shared by cloud consumers,
  direct use of owned material depth, and reconciliation of DepthHierarchyRenderer's primary-depth
  acquisition. Inventory unrelated integer inputs without migrating them.
  Preserve render order, shader math, visibility rejection, sampler policy, content publication and
  failure behavior. No texture copies, parallel state/validation caches, per-frame metadata queries,
  integer-keyed global resource registry, new shader algorithm or general engine interception.
  Use subagents for every build and test tool invocation. User-run live appearance and frame-time
  acceptance are separate from source, focused GPU and headless evidence. Never launch the game
  for visual acceptance. This file is a planning artifact; creating it does not begin implementation.

Open questions and decision gates
  ☐ Establish whether existing native framebuffer hooks cover rebuild entry, successful/failed
    completion, same-size replacement, reused names and teardown before any intervening borrow.
    ScreenResourceManager's post-rebuild callback alone does not certify advance retirement.
  ☐ Determine how the current producer integration prevents or rejects native mutation during a
    synchronous borrow; managed resource guards alone cannot prevent external deletion.
  ☐ Identify which borrowed inputs, if any, can also be image-bound so retirement covers those slots.
  ☐ Document any unsupported lifecycle path and retain conservative handling for its inputs. Report
    the coverage gap before expanding integration scope. A safe fallback is not evidence that the
    affected optimization or the parent task is complete; unresolved scope requires explicit review.

Phase 1: Establish lifecycle coverage and a matched baseline
  Outcome: eligible inputs, integration boundaries and comparison conditions are known before changes.

  ☐ Reconcile the complete proposal with current source and repository instructions.
    - Inspect ShaderPreparedSubmission, ShaderInputValidation, BorrowedTexture, GpuResource,
      StateCache texture/image slots, lifetime retirement and engine-boundary restoration.
    - Inspect cloud Simple and volume consumers, CloudMapPublication/CloudNativeMapAdapter,
      DepthHierarchyRenderer, ScreenResourceManager and actual native framebuffer replacement paths.
    - Record current raw-ID versus typed inputs; do not assume volume bindings still use integers.
  ☐ Record each in-scope input's producer, allocation/context identity, metadata authority, acquisition,
    replacement/retirement notifications, content qualification and synchronous borrowing interval.
    - Verify ordering from producer source, including failure, late acquisition, reload, world/context
      teardown, same-size replacement and handle reuse. Resolve the open questions above with evidence.
    - Keep unproven inputs conservative and identify the exact missing observation boundary.
  ☐ Establish a reproducible baseline before modifying runtime behavior.
    - Record source identity, relevant existing local changes, build configuration, fixture inputs,
      cloud modes, settings, hardware/context and available measurement tooling.
    - Have subagents run relevant existing focused GPU tests and the canonical build/package path
      applicable to the touched contracts; record failures rather than attributing them to later edits.
    - Capture Simple and volumetric actual texture binds, cache checks, metadata queries, compatibility
      checks, cloud outputs and restored GL state, including boundary entry/exit costs.
    - Retain identical inputs and a reproducible baseline executable/source for the final comparison.
  ☐ Completion: every proposed migration has verified lifecycle coverage or a documented conservative
    disposition; baseline receipts and fixture conditions permit an attributable before/after comparison.

Phase 2: Express borrowed allocation retirement through existing resource machinery
  Depends on Phase 1's lifecycle coverage and baseline.
  Outcome: a borrowed allocation can be retained and revoked without deleting external storage.

  ☐ Retain one immutable BorrowedTexture metadata wrapper for each admitted external allocation.
    - Tie admission to producer owner, allocation generation and graphics context generation.
    - Query metadata only after successful allocation validation; replace rather than retarget the
      wrapper on allocation change. Dispose/invalidate the old reference so retained inputs fail.
    - Unknown late acquisition or unobserved replacement withdraws tracked eligibility. Do not
      certify identity using only dimensions, framebuffer reference, native ID or GL.IsTexture.
  ☐ Extend existing StateCache lifetime handling only as needed for external withdrawal.
    - Forget all texture associations for the retired native name and applicable image associations
      before the next cache observation/restoration. Preserve unrelated knowledge where justified.
    - Record retirement for saved boundary restoration so it cannot bind a retired allocation or a
      same-name replacement. Do not equate withdrawal with GL's implicit unbinding on deletion.
    - Preserve existing broad invalidation after uncertain native rebuild/context state changes.
    - Keep owned deletion behavior and external metadata retirement distinct; borrowed retirement
      performs no native deletion, queued deletion or handle transfer.
  ☐ Preserve synchronous borrow guards, complete-input validation and failure cleanup.
    - Check current eligibility during validation and at publication if intervening activity is possible.
    - Prevent or reconcile attempted external replacement before mutation while borrows are active.
    - Reuse ShaderInputValidation resource identity; no second compatibility cache. Ensure retirement
      and replacement cannot inherit a previous successful validation or partially publish inputs.
  ☐ Have subagents run focused GPU lifetime/cache coverage before consumer migration.
    - Exercise same-name replacement, unrelated-slot preservation, applicable image slots, active
      borrows, exception cleanup and restoration retirement; verify native borrowed textures survive.
    - Confirm raw-ID validation/invalidation and owned texture deletion remain conservative/correct.
  ☐ Completion: revoked references reject use and restoration, native ownership is preserved, and
    cache invalidation is correct independently of cloud consumer integration.

Phase 3: Retain native cloud-map metadata under allocation certificates
  Depends on Phase 2.
  Outcome: cloud-map metadata survives content updates but never outlives its admitted allocation.

  ☐ Place persistent map-reference authority in the existing producer-observing cloud integration.
    - Withdraw before native allocation mutation via the existing allocation-entry observation;
      admit a fresh wrapper only after validated successful completion.
    - Reuse allocation certificates and retire references on owner/world/context teardown.
    - Keep upload, shader and completed generation certificates independent of allocation identity.
  ☐ Replace per-callback map-wrapper acquisition in CloudStoredVolumeConsumer with the eligible
    producer reference, preserving synchronous borrowing and existing same-frame Current checks.
    - In-place uploads preserve allocation metadata but invalidate/republish content as before.
    - Reload refreshes executable/content validity as required without fabricating storage replacement.
  ☐ Have subagents verify repeated valid use, stale-frame rejection, failed upload/generation/allocation,
    reload, late acquisition, native replacement and same-name reuse through focused tests.
    - Confirm missing content certificates reject rendering even while allocation metadata remains live.
    - Verify metadata acquisition occurs on admitted allocation changes rather than each callback.
  ☐ Completion: map wrappers follow allocation lifetime, publication freshness is unchanged, and no
    native storage ownership or asynchronous retention has been introduced.

Phase 4: Share qualified depth references across existing consumers
  Depends on Phase 2 and Phase 1's verified depth lifecycle disposition; follows Phase 3 for integration.
  Outcome: eligible native depth inputs retain metadata; owned material depth is used directly.

  ☐ Pass the existing VGE-owned DynamicTexture2D material-depth result directly to volume view inputs.
    - Preserve TryGetCurrent frame, camera-view and extent qualification and producer disposal authority.
  ☐ Retain eligible native primary/visibility and liquid-depth wrappers in their owning rendering
    integration. Reuse references across compatible cloud and DepthHierarchyRenderer consumers.
    - Withdraw on verified replacement entry; deny borrowing during replacement; admit only validated
      completion. Preserve ScreenResourceManager reconciliation and dependency ordering.
    - Cover native target replacement independently of dimension or integer-ID changes.
    - Reconcile the existing retained primary-depth acquisition with this same lifecycle contract.
  ☐ Convert eligible Simple liquid-depth publication to the existing typed texture binding contract;
    remove redundant volume depth wrapper creation only where qualified references replace it.
    - Preserve native sampler policy, shader declarations, scene qualification and visible render order.
    - Keep unproven paths conservative; remove no necessary raw-ID fallback.
  ☐ Have subagents run focused integration tests for both cloud modes and shared-depth acquisition.
    - Exercise resize, same-size replacement, handle reuse, failed rebuild, reload, world/context
      changes and stale depth frame/view/extent. Verify old references cannot publish or restore.
    - Exercise intervening native binding changes and unknown slots: live allocation identity must
      not suppress a necessary bind. Confirm unchanged known slot state permits suppression.
  ☐ Completion: migrated depth consumers use correctly qualified references, raw untracked paths
    remain safe, and lifetime/content/engine-boundary contracts hold together.

Phase 5: Qualify correctness and actual work reduction
  Depends on Phases 3 and 4; no candidate edits during matched measurements.
  Outcome: an immutable candidate has attributable correctness and work-count receipts.

  ☐ Review the integrated change against every proposal requirement before freezing the candidate.
    - Resolve unintended scope, ownership, callback ordering or failure-path changes first.
    - Have subagents run applicable focused GPU regression coverage and canonical build/package checks
      for typed binding/generator changes. Record exact commands, candidate identity and results.
  ☐ Compare frozen baseline/candidate using identical scenes, inputs, settings and configurations.
    - Report Simple and volume actual binds separately from cache checks, metadata queries and
      compatibility checks; include boundary restoration costs and initial acquisition costs.
    - Demonstrate fewer actual binds on eligible repeated known-slot publication and no redundant
      metadata acquisition for unchanged tracked allocations. Report zero savings where applicable.
    - Compare cloud outputs, visibility rejection and restored texture/sampler/GL state, including
      native interference, replacement, retirement and exception paths. Explain any discrepancy.
    - Confirm raw-ID uses still force conservative binding and owned disposal retains its semantics.
  ☐ Distinguish fixture correctness, measured call/query savings, live appearance and frame time.
    - Provide user-run visual scenarios for both cloud modes, movement/visibility, resize and reload.
      Record user acceptance or leave it explicitly outstanding; do not launch the game yourself.
    - Collect matched user-run timing evidence only for performance claims; fewer calls do not prove
      GPU/frame-time improvement. No timing claim is required merely to report verified bind savings.
  ☐ Completion: required focused/build/package checks pass, unchanged tested output/state is evidenced,
    and actual bind/query savings are separately supported. Missing live evidence remains explicit.

Phase 6: Reconcile documentation and completion authority
  Depends on Phase 5's qualified candidate and evidence.
  Outcome: documentation describes active behavior and checklist status matches demonstrated results.

  ☐ Update Rendering.ShaderBindingState.md and any affected permanent ownership/lifecycle documents
    to describe actual tracked-input eligibility, retirement/restoration and raw integer behavior.
    - Remove stale blanket statements about borrowed handles without weakening untracked safeguards.
      Keep historical/staging narratives out of permanent architecture documentation.
    - Link reproducible qualification receipts from planning/evidence artifacts; remove temporary
      diagnostics/scaffolding not needed by retained tests or supported measurement tools.
  ☐ Perform a second review and independent audit against the complete approved proposal, this plan,
    current implementation, linked documentation and frozen-candidate receipts. Reconcile suspected
    discrepancies against the full contracts before reporting or closing them.
    - If fixes change the measured candidate, rerun affected checks/comparisons before claiming closure.
    - Do not treat a documented fallback or an unrelated input inventory as a completed migration.
  ☐ Reconcile the BaselineShading parent task with actual qualified scope and outstanding acceptance.
    - Keep the parent open until agreed correctness and actual bind-saving requirements are met.
      Retain unresolved lifecycle/visual acceptance work explicitly; scope reductions need review.
  ☐ Completion: implementation, evidence and documentation agree; remaining limitations and live
    evidence boundaries are explicit, and no later or unverified work is marked complete.

Final acceptance
  ☐ Every proposal requirement is implemented and verified or has an explicitly reviewed disposition;
    conservative fallback does not silently substitute for the intended optimization.
  ☐ Producer allocation/context lifetime, content freshness, binding state, borrowed ownership,
    restoration safety and raw-ID compatibility each have appropriate evidence.
  ☐ Matched measurements demonstrate actual bind savings and separately report metadata savings.
  ☐ Required checks, documentation, second review, independent audit and agreed user-run acceptance
    are complete; frame-time claims are limited to measured timing evidence.
